{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjQBKEbt76RPGBDQX6Bh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote all three files for the repair, and the new model passes the self-check (`OK`). It should no longer hit the 30-minute limit, and it converged in a test fit. It also fits much worse than the current best model, so it will most likely lose the comparison.\n\n**Mechanism (unchanged):** a sequence is judged by its single most suspicious stretch. For every contiguous run of three or more flips, the model computes how much better a regular generator explains it than a fair coin. The regular generators are a coin biased towards switching, a coin biased towards one face, and a short motif copied with slips. A soft maximum over those stretches decides how non-random the whole sequence looks. This is what the brief asked for: one striking local stretch decides, not an average over the sequence.\n\n**What made it slow, and the fix:** the old version gave each of 400 participants their own belief about how often a fair coin switches. That meant the 21 stretches had to be scored again for every one of the 5,120 trials, and the person-level parameters were weakly pinned down. I made that belief a single shared value. Now each distinct sequence's worst stretch is computed once and looked up per trial, and only four parameters remain. I also dropped the participant-id input, which nothing used any more.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Self-check:** passed; the 200-iteration smoke fit sampled in 32 s.\n- **Convergence test:** 4 chains of 500 warm-up plus 500 draws took 28 s, with 0 divergent transitions, R-hat 1.0 and bulk ESS of at least 884. Admission needs no more than 0.1% divergent, R-hat ≤ 1.05 and ESS ≥ 100.\n- **Fit:** the smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −2754, against −2103 for the current best. Dropping person-specific switch beliefs costs fit, and the stretch-maximum idea has done poorly before: `longest_patterned_stretc